WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.925
Most civilian cloud systems are authorized
at Moderate, and the word does real work.

2
00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:10.260
Under FIPS 199, the agency rates the
impact of losing confidentiality,

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.675
integrity and availability
for each kind of information.

4
00:00:14.675 --> 00:00:16.876
FIPS 200 takes the high-water mark:

5
00:00:16.876 --> 00:00:21.750
at least one objective at moderate and
none at high makes a moderate system.

6
00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.032
The agency then selects and
tailors NIST's Moderate baseline,

7
00:00:26.032 --> 00:00:30.650
which in the current release holds
287 controls and enhancements.

8
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.762
That's the size of the promise
a Moderate system makes.

9
00:00:33.762 --> 00:00:36.500
FedRAMP's Class C is a separate fact.

10
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.695
It includes the current Moderate
baseline, but FedRAMP lists 322 controls

11
00:00:41.695 --> 00:00:46.387
and enhancements for it, 36 of them
outside NIST's Moderate baseline.

12
00:00:46.387 --> 00:00:50.361
Automated access enforcement
for changes, for example,

13
00:00:50.361 --> 00:00:53.815
is NIST High only, yet
FedRAMP lists it for Class C.

14
00:00:53.815 --> 00:00:56.838
Non-repudiation is High
only on both lists.

15
00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:58.575
So cite the two separately.

16
00:00:58.575 --> 00:01:01.769
A Moderate system built on
a Class C service inherits

17
00:01:01.769 --> 00:01:04.600
the provider's evidence
for the provider's side.

18
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.100
It still has to prove its own side,
against its own tailored baseline.
